535461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535911 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535911 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535911 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186535461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535911 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d3ccd5f4-a37a-56a2-9400-43d3c5f9830f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186535802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186535802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535918 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ef28899d-b306-5e8e-a4d3-bef109a6e779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535929 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535929 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535929 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535929 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+2x$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535929 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fbb26d4-3faf-5389-98e4-54735a0cfc0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535929 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0b/0b9dd2388a7b60866eb15d336d51d181853fe5ac0e52dcd3e02f53fdd4848532.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535930 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535930 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535930 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535930 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10bc8e6f-4c2c-5a63-aa64-53c9dbd12a9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535930 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/5c/5c52bbe248df0e5a595298d978276023bcb681daa904f37e2818013687aab52e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.75$ | $3$ | $1.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.75+3+1.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =8.50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535933 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535933 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535933 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535933 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[1,7].$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2.5$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $5$ | $8.5$ | $9$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,7].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535933 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16561015-1e48-5ef8-9194-b46c8f058fda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535933 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(2.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(5)Δ{x}_{2}+f(7)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2.5$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $5$ | $8.5$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈5\\cdot 1.5+8.5\\cdot 2.5+9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.5+21.25+18 \\\\  & =46.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535934 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535934 #edb/ref 1069 "q-316517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535934 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535934 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the water flow rate, $R(t),$ through a pipe (in liters per minute) over a period of $40$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R\\,\\text{(liters/minute)}$ | $12$ | $15$ | $17$ | $16$ | $14$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,40]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of water pumped.\n\nThe total volume is \n{{field-1}} liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535934 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18df9f2e-656d-5486-9ae1-27119d2f448d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535934 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=40,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{40-0}{4}=10.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(10)+R(20)+R(30)+R(40)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $15$ | $17$ | $16$ | $14$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(15+17+16+14)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =62\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =620\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume of water pumped over a period of $40$ minutes. So, the total volume is $620$ liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535941 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535941 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535941 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535941 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the cooling rate of a walk-in freezer over a period of $10$ hours, starting at $9\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ | $15$ | $17$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{kilojoules/hour})$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\n If the right Riemann sum approximation of the total energy removed from $9\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $40$ kilojoules, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535941 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "295aa24d-cc18-5ddf-b640-8d50f96f1e1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535941 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(11)+R(13)+R(15)+R(17)+R(19)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $11$ | $13$ | $15$ | $17$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(4+3+2+4+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+13) \\\\  & =2k+26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total energy removed from $9\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total energy removed is $40$ kilojoules, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+26 & =40 \\\\ 2k & =40-26 \\\\ k & =\\frac{14}{2} \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535943 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535943 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535943 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535943 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the fuel consumption rate, $F(t),$ of a generator (in liters per hour) over a period of $8$ hours.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(hours)}$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F\\,\\text{(liters/hour)}$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $F(t)$ over the interval $[0,8]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total amount of fuel used.\n\nThe total amount of fuel is \n{{field-1}} liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e5afc97-cdbf-5609-994c-66b16cc595b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535943 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=8,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{8-0}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{F(2)+F(4)+F(6)+F(8)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $F(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(t)$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+5+2+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total amount of fuel used over a period of $8$ hours. So, the total amount of fuel is $32$ liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535955 #edb/ref 1071 "A drone has positive velocity $v(t)$ after being flown upward from an initial height of $0$ feet at time  $t=0$ seconds. The velocity of the drone is recorded for selected values of $t$ over the interval $0\\le t\\le 40$ seconds, as shown in the table below. \n\n| $t\\,\\text{(seconds)}$ | $0$ | $7$ | $15$ | $25$ | $32$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v\\,\\text{(feet/second)}$ | $3$ | $10$ | $25$ | $40$ | $46$ | $49$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,40]$ with five subintervals to approximate the total distance covered by the drone.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6772ccf8-308a-5e57-b20e-bbe6b51388bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535955 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=v(t)$ over $[0,40].$ \nIn this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the total distance can be approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈v(7)Δ{t}_{1}+v(15)Δ{t}_{2}+v(25)Δ{t}_{3}+v(32)Δ{t}_{4}+v(40)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $7$ | $15$ | $25$ | $32$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $7$ | $8$ | $10$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| $v(t)$ | $-$ | $10$ | $25$ | $40$ | $46$ | $49$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the number of liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈10\\cdot 7+25\\cdot 8+40\\cdot 10+46\\cdot 7+49\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =70+200+400+322+392 \\\\  & =1\\,384.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance covered by the drone. So, the distance is approximately $1\\,384$ feet." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535960 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the rate $r(t)$, in flour bags per minute, at which a machine makes flour over a period of $75$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $15$ | $30$ | $45$ | $60$ | $75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r\\,\\text{(flour bags/minute)}$ | $30$ | $25$ | $35$ | $40$ | $35$ | $25$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $r(t)$ over the interval $[0,75]$, with five subintervals, to approximate the number of flour bags made by the machine in $75$ minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7984804e-ee09-5ab8-9ea3-cfb9bf84394d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535960 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=75,$ and there are $n=5$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{75-0}{5}=15.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r(15)+r(30)+r(45)+r(60)+r(75)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $15$ | $30$ | $45$ | $60$ | $75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $25$ | $35$ | $40$ | $35$ | $25$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+35+40+35+25)\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =160\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =2\\,400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of flour bags made by the machine in $75$ minutes.  So, the number of flour bags is approximately $2\\,400.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535962 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535962 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535962 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535962 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives the medication infusion rate $r(t)$, in milligrams per minute, for a patient.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,\\text{(mg/min)}$ | $6$ | $5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,18]$ with five subintervals to approximate the total medication delivered (in milligrams).\n\nThe total medication delivered is\n{{field-1}} milligrams." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b623f34-55bd-5cf9-90b7-bc3a51b954e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535962 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=r(t)$ over $[0,18].$ In this case, we have $n=5$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is computed as \n\n$$\nA≈r(4)Δ{t}_{1}+r(7)Δ{t}_{2}+r(10)Δ{t}_{3}+r(13)Δ{t}_{4}+r(18)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| $r(t)$ | $-$ | $5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈5\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =20+9+12+6+5 \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total accumulation of medication. So, the total medication delivered is $52$ milligrams." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535963 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535963 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535963 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table gives the speed of a minivan measured during a $4$-second interval.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{seconds})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{meters/second})$ | $17$ | $18$ | $k$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\n If the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance that the minivan traveled during the $4$-second interval has a value of $85$ meters, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86b94149-e5f8-5f16-b1b9-921b18ee69eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535963 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v(1)+v(2)+v(3)+v(4)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $18$ | $k$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(18+k+25+27)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =70+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance that the minivan traveled. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $85$ meters,  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70+k & =85 \\\\ k & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535967 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the water drainage rate of a reservoir over a period of $8$ hours, starting at $2\\,\\text{p.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{gallons/hour})$ | $2$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $30$ gallons, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8de05636-df00-52b0-a8f6-5c5d3db73b2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535967 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(4)+R(6)+R(8)+R(10)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+12) \\\\  & =2k+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total drainage is $30$ gallons, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+24 & =30 \\\\ 2k & =30-24 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535975 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=2x+3$ over the interval $[-1,1]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e633987-af32-5b5c-bd64-b66c42ee580f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535975 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/be/be5f6207531c18acecdb6a7ba3890e23913de4a78546db620f2986dd0467fa6d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-0.5)+f(0)+f(0.5)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+3+4+5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =14\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[-1,1],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74350" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535982 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6].$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $8$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae6e559e-361b-5b37-89a9-1d035fa07b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535982 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(3.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(6)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $0.5$ | $2$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 1.5+3.5\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+1.75+16 \\\\  & =20.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535985 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535985 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535985 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535985 #edb/ref 1071 "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=1$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *underestimate* of the area?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535985 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7189171-aff4-5525-9ffc-edd7b878fae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535985 #edb/ref 1074 "The right Riemann sum underestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is decreasing over $x\\in [-2,1]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/12/1287968881b822c475474e0a1e89ed589473d24f7cdf278c6d9fbd18b960344b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535995 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535995 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[0,15]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $60$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d36beb24-ba8a-5d0f-80e1-d03ab3463ddd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535995 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(5)+f(10)+f(15)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+4+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(7+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $60,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =60 \\\\ 5k & =60-35 \\\\ k & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536003 #edb/ref 1071 "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=0$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *underestimate* of the area?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f185512a-6bdc-56ba-b826-59b4b54f5782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536003 #edb/ref 1074 "The right Riemann sum underestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is decreasing over $x\\in [-2,0]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/54/54da2c81b258a8b4768ff8550e261c1f8997c64df40e83160e674d9d223c601e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536005 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(t)$ on the closed interval $[10,40].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(t)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $480.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $t$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $18$ | $12$ | $k$ | $16$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbb4c94f-2803-5b7e-b57a-776b92b59056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536005 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(20)+f(30)+f(40)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $12$ | $k$ | $16$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(12+k+16)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =(28+k)\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $480$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(28+k)\\cdot 10 & =480 \\\\ 28+k & =48 \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
